> Robert R Schneck <schneck@math.berkeley.edu> wrote in message
> news:<psQqc.83$Zx3.3811@petpeeve.ziplink.net>...
> > 
> > It looks to me like when gremlins steal protection, they don't reset the 
> > AC count, but just remove the protection intrinsic itself.  If so, it 
> > should all come back as soon as you get any protection... including from 
> > a cloak or ring.

Only from eating a ring, from donation, or from a prayer boon:
u.ublessed requires _intrinsic_ protection to be applied to AC,
extrinsic or timeout won't suffice.

> Actually, it's the other way around... There is a flag HProtection,
> and a counter u.ublessed.  When a gremlin steals your protection,
> u.ublessed is set to 0, but HProtection is not changed.

Wrong. Robert is quite correct; from attrcurse():

        case 10: if (HProtection & INTRINSIC) {
                        HProtection &= ~INTRINSIC;
                        You_feel("vulnerable.");
                        break;
                }

> > However, if your naked AC is above 1, the priest should be giving you 
> > the protection... that I can't understand.
>
> This way, you only get the 2-4 points of protection from your first
> 400*XL donation to a priest, not just when u.ublessed = 0.

Both conditions are necessary to get the introductory special offer.
If you've lost protection while u.ublessed is greater than 0, all that
happens is that protection is restored (and so u.ublessed starts being
applied to actual AC again), you _don't_ get the additional point. (I
believe this to be a bug, and submitted it as such to the DevTeam on
2001-08-13.)

                if (!(HProtection & INTRINSIC))  {
                        HProtection |= FROMOUTSIDE;
                        if (!u.ublessed)  u.ublessed = rn1(3, 2);
                } else u.ublessed++;

